sec.p:
The raw signals acquired during the Robot-Assisted Gait Training sessions were processed using a standardized feature extraction pipeline, aimed at enabling a multimodal assessment of the physiological and motor responses associated with the patient’s participation state. After multimodal synchronization, all signals were segmented into consecutive, non-overlapping 30-s windows. No overlap was applied between adjacent windows, and no specific portions of the recordings were excluded. Lokomat® signals were acquired after device calibration and patient setup; therefore, calibration, positioning, and preparation phases were not included in the analysed data. Within each window, a structured and homogeneous set of descriptive numerical features was computed, designed to capture statistical, temporal, and physiological characteristics relevant to the objectives of the study. For HRV, standard time- and frequency-domain features commonly employed to assess autonomic nervous system activity were extracted. Specifically, mean RR, representing the average duration of inter-beat intervals, SDNN, reflecting overall heart rate variability, and RMSSD, which is sensitive to short-term variations predominantly mediated by parasympathetic activity, were computed. In addition, spectral features were derived, including low-frequency (LF) power, high-frequency (HF) power, and the LF/HF ratio, which is commonly interpreted as an index of sympatho–vagal balance (Table 1). Facial IRT analysis was performed on five regions of interest (ROIs): nose, nostrils, chin, perioral area, and corrugator region. To ensure accurate and consistent ROI positioning over time, an RGB camera was simultaneously used during data acquisition to record the patient’s face. These videos were subsequently processed using the OpenFace software for automatic detection of facial landmarks [42]. Since RGB and thermal images were acquired from two distinct optical sensors, a spatial co-registration between the visible and thermal views was first performed. For this purpose, a set of corresponding calibration points was selected on a representative RGB–thermal frame pair and used to estimate an affine geometrical transformation between the two image planes. This transformation was independent of the facial landmarks used for ROI tracking. Let \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
