For each thermal frame, the temporally corresponding visible frame was identified by minimizing the absolute time difference between the RGB and thermal time vectors. The mapped thermal landmarks were then used to initialize and dynamically update the facial ROIs. Circular ROIs with a fixed diameter of 9 pixels were centred on selected thermal landmarks for the nose, right nostril, and left nostril regions, whereas the chin ROI was centred on the midpoint between the corresponding chin and lower-lip landmarks. Polygonal ROIs were defined for the perioral and corrugator regions using the corresponding landmark contours (Fig. 3). Specifically, the nose, right nostril, and left nostril ROIs were centred on landmarks 31, 33, and 35, respectively; the chin ROI was centred on the midpoint between landmarks 9 and 58; the perioral ROI was defined using landmarks 32–36 and 49–55; and the corrugator ROI using landmarks 22–23 and 28. Thermal features were extracted separately from the right and left nostril ROIs and subsequently averaged feature-wise, yielding a single nostril-related feature set used in the machine-learning analysis. Importantly, this procedure was used only for landmark and ROI coordinate transfer; no pixel-wise warping or deformation of the thermal frames was applied for feature extraction. From each ROI, several features were extracted to characterize both the statistical dynamics and autonomic modulation of the thermal signals. These included mean temperature (T_mean), indicating overall thermal level; standard deviation (T_std), as a measure of local variability; skewness (T_skew) and kurtosis (T_kurt), describing the shape of the temperature distribution; and sample entropy (SampEn), used as an index of temporal complexity and irregularity. Furthermore, to investigate autonomic components of the thermal response, frequency-domain features were computed, including LF and HF components and the LF/HF ratio (Table 1). In addition to physiological signals, kinematic information was extracted from the torque signals provided by the Lokomat ®. Before feature extraction, torque signals were pre-processed by applying a second-order polynomial detrending procedure to remove slow-varying offsets, followed by fourth-order low-pass filtering with a cut-off frequency of 8 Hz. For each window, the absolute value of each joint torque signal was numerically integrated using the trapezoidal rule:4\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
