To reduce model complexity, enhance interpretability, and maintain strict separation between training and test data, a nested feature selection procedure was embedded within each outer validation split. Specifically, for every outer training partition, an inner cross-validation loop was performed exclusively on the training data to identify the optimal number of retained features. Within each inner fold, features were ranked using the impurity-based feature importance scores, corresponding to Mean Decrease in Impurity (MDI). Permutation importance was used as a model-agnostic measure of feature relevance. For each trained model, the values of one feature at a time were randomly shuffled in the held-out test set, whereas all other features were left unchanged. The decrease in macro-F1 score after permutation was then computed; larger decreases indicated a stronger contribution of that feature to model performance. In this study, permutation importance was computed on the test set of each outer split using macro-F1 as the scoring metric and 20 permutation repeats. Let \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
